  73: 
  74: ## Architectural decisions
  75: 
  76: ### ADR-001 â€” Canonical order and immutable source identity
  77: Context: AI output and published versions must remain traceable through corrections.
  78: Decision: approve SourceRevision into immutable source-specific canonical text; seq is book order and IDs begin p000000. Store sourceId/hash on derived work.
  79: Consequence: a new source invalidates dependent drafts; published snapshots and prior source remain intact.
  80: 
  81: ### ADR-002 â€” Complete-book representation
  82: Context: a cinematic presentation can silently omit information even when all source text is retained.
  83: Decision: every story paragraph has a meaningful Representation in one or more Beats. Preserve internal/abstract meaning in HTML subtitles when visuals cannot carry it; no story gaps or overlapping Episode/Moment ownership.
  84: Consequence: deterministic coverage and evidence checks plus semantic/editorial review are publication gates; source retention alone is insufficient.
  85: 
  86: ### ADR-003 â€” AI plans; owner approves and generates visuals
  87: Context: the owner explicitly rejects manual prompts, structured-data entry, camera direction and layer authoring.
  88: Decision: AI generates all planning and camera/transition/timing choices; the owner selects single/multiple visual targets, previews and approves or requests regeneration.
  89: Consequence: guided next actions and bounded automatic plan repair replace required editing forms.
  90: 
  91: ### ADR-004 â€” Typed shared contracts
  92: Context: the brief includes conflicting production and prototype fixture types.
  93: Decision: Zod schemas are the single source of runtime validation and inferred types; richer production forms, representations, source/reference versions and publication semantics win.
  94: Consequence: APIs, worker, codecs, fixtures, previews and bundles share contracts; there is no separate Reader code/type copy here.
  95: 
  96: ### ADR-005 â€” Trusted writes and development-only bypass
  97: Context: browser reads need Firebase readiness while current server-only development bypass leaves a login dependency.
  98: Decision: make development usable without interactive sign-in; keep production auth and trusted server writes enforced, with no permissive live rules or exposed credentials.
  99: Consequence: auth/data readiness is an isolated build phase and must pass production-negative tests. No auth change was applied during planning.
 100: 
 101: ### ADR-006 â€” Durable individual jobs
 102: Context: long jobs require resumability and the allJobs map is not an unbounded queue.
 103: Decision: one Firestore document per Job; transactional claims/leases, checkpointed units, idempotent charges, bounded concurrency and explicit visual-generation triggers.
 104: Consequence: approval-driven planning can resume after failures without duplicate paid work.
 105: 
 106: ### ADR-007 â€” Reference-first generated scenes
 107: Context: visual consistency requires approved shared identity and state across many scenes.
 108: Decision: approve versioned visualProfile and entity/state/location references before scene generation; pin those versions and review continuity.
 109: Consequence: regenerate failures, never assume reference conditioning guarantees exact identity, and flag dependent drafts when references change.
 110: 
 111: ### ADR-008 â€” Generated layers, automatic assembly
 112: Context: the owner does not want dedicated depth/segmentation/inpainting services or manual asset correction.
 113: Decision: image generation produces clean backgrounds, aligned alpha layers and optional depth; internal assembly supports planes and depthMesh, validates coverage and computes safeCamera.
 114: Consequence: no third-party 2.5D provider; missing/invalid depth blocks depthMesh approval, with targeted regeneration available.
 115: 
 116: ### ADR-009 â€” HTML subtitles and faithful preview
 117: Context: text must remain readable across devices and the external Reader needs a consistent contract.
 118: Decision: render text as HTML over the composition; preview Beat/Moment/Episode from the same bundle contract, including CSS fallback and reduced motion.
 119: Consequence: no baked text or required camera controls; partial placeholders are honest draft states and never publishable.
 120: 
 121: ### ADR-010 â€” AWS assets and immutable episode publication
 122: Context: finished Episodes must be available before the whole book and remain stable during regeneration.
 123: Decision: all generated assets/manifests/bundles live in S3/CloudFront; source/canonical/ledger stay Firebase Storage; Firestore owns metadata/pointers.
 124: Consequence: stage versioned output, atomically promote active pointers/catalog, keep old versions for active sessions and rollback, and never mutate shared published assets.
 125: 
 126: ### ADR-011 â€” Source of truth for reuse and revisions
 127: Context: duplicate composition links and mutable reference IDs create drift.
 128: Decision: Composition.usedIn owns reuse relationships; Moment.compositionIds is derived. Working revisions and published snapshots pin source/style/reference/asset versions.
 129: Consequence: regeneration invalidates only affected draft dependencies and cannot silently alter another published Episode.
 130: 
 131: ### ADR-012 â€” Local implementation, owner-only release
 132: Context: the owner permits code/test automation but reserves paid generation, deployment and live publication.
 133: Decision: implementation uses deterministic fakes/local fixtures; P13 is HUMAN-ONLY. No cloud migration/deletion is needed; no consumer Reader, payment or distribution work.
 134: Consequence: artistic/model capability and deployed security remain explicit owner QA, never inferred from local tests.

